WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.850
<v Stephane>So now let's talk about EBS volumes,</v>

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.290
and there are different volume types.

3
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.390
They come in six different types today,

4
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.760
and we can group them in several categories.

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.160
The first one is gp2/gp3,

6
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.410
which is a general purpose SSD volume

7
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.550
that will balance price

8
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.040
and performance for a wide variety of workloads.

9
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.180
And this is something we've been using

10
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.650
so far in this course.

11
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.470
Then we have io1 and io2 Block Express.

12
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.170
They're highest-performance SSD volumes that will be used

13
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.760
for mission-critical low-latency

14
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.100
and high-throughput workloads.

15
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.900
Then we have st 1 volumes

16
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.880
and they're low-cost HDD volume designed

17
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.000
for frequently accessed throughput-intensive workloads.

18
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.200
We have sc 1 volume,

19
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.960
which are going to be the lowest cost HDD volume,

20
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.040
and they will be designed

21
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.560
for less frequently accessed workloads.

22
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.960
Now, how do you define an EBS volume?

23
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.200
Well, you can have several factors, for example, size,

24
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.380
throughputs, and IOPS, which means I/O,

25
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.570
operations per second.

26
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.040
And obviously when in doubt,

27
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.530
always consult the documentation.

28
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.047
Now, for your EC2 instances, only gp2 and gp3

29
00:01:03.047 --> 00:01:06.060
and io1 and io2 can be used as boot volumes.

30
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.720
That means where the root OS is going to be running.

31
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.890
So let's do a deeper dive

32
00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.410
into the different EBS volume types.

33
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.740
The first one is the General Purpose or GP SSD.

34
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.920
So we have the cost effective storage and low latency.

35
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.430
They can be used as system boots volumes, virtual desktops,

36
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.880
Development and test environments, medium sized databases,

37
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.650
and transactional workloads.

38
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.730
We have the gp3 type of volume, which gives you a baseline

39
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:37.170
of 3,000 IOPS and a throughput of 125 megabytes per second.

40
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.040
And you can increase the IOPS up to 80,000

41
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.170
and the throughput up to 2,000 megabytes

42
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.210
per second independently.

43
00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.020
The gp2 volume will give you small gp2 volumes

44
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.660
that can burst up to 3,000 IOPS,

45
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.010
and there is the size and the IOPS capacity that are linked.

46
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.430
So as you increase the size of your gp2 volume,

47
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.960
the IOPS will increase

48
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.147
and the max IOPS you can ever get is 16,000.

49
00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:06.900
So in gp3, you can set them independently.

50
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.450
In gp2, they are linked together.

51
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.250
You get 3 IOPS per gigabyte.

52
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.250
That means that at 5,300 something gigabytes,

53
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.873
we are at the max IOPS.

54
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.560
Next we get the Provisioned IOPS SSD.

55
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.590
So this is when you have a critical business application

56
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.720
with sustained IOPS performance

57
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.140
or applications that will need more than 80,000 IOPS.

58
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.380
This is great for very good database workloads

59
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.400
that will be sensitive

60
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.160
to storage performance and consistency.

61
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.470
So we have the io1 volume where you get

62
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.970
a max IOPS of 64,000.

63
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.613
PIOPS is Provisioned IOPS.

64
00:02:43.088 --> 00:02:44.730
64,000 for Nitro EC2 instances

65
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.440
and 32,000 for others.

66
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.760
And you can increase the IOPS independently

67
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.803
from the storage size with a ratio of about 50 to 1.

68
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.640
And the io2 Block Express volumes

69
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.430
give you sub-millisecond latency,

70
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.670
give you a much greater IOPS max capacity.

71
00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.780
So 256,000 IOPS with a ratio

72
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.620
of 1,000 IOPS per gigabyte maximum.

73
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.683
Also, they support EBS Multi-attach feature.

74
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.020
So now let's quickly touch upon st1 and sc1.

75
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.370
So they cannot be a boot volumes,

76
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.680
this is only for the previous types of volumes.

77
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.590
You can get the size up to 16 terabytes

78
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.140
and we get two kinds of volumes here.

79
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.260
We have the throughput optimized HDD, so st1,

80
00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.270
which is great for big data, data warehousing,

81
00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.030
and log processing, which gives you a max throughput

82
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.870
of 500 megabytes per second and a max IOPS of 500.

83
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.240
And then for cold HDD so, sc1,

84
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.800
this is for archive data,

85
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.440
so data that is infrequently accessed.

86
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.360
And this is when you would use it when you need

87
00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.100
to have the lowest possible cost.

88
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.370
Here, the max throughput is 250 megabytes per second

89
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.860
and the max IOPS is 250 as well.

90
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.090
Now you don't have to remember all

91
00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.650
these details going into the exam.

92
00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.940
You just need to understand the differences

93
00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.070
at a high level of all these volumes.

94
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.760
So the General Purpose SSD versus the Provisioned IOPS SSD

95
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.830
in case you need a database versus

96
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.840
some st1 and sc1 in case you need

97
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.870
to have high throughput and lowest cost.

98
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.430
And so you can find a summary

99
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.670
of all I said in this link right here, okay?

100
00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.410
Which is the screenshot I just took you.

101
00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.720
So don't need to remember the details again,

102
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.440
but remember that if you wanna get also over 32,000 IOPS,

103
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.130
you need EC2 Nitro with io1 or io2.

104
00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.900
And that will be it for this lecture.

105
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.770
So I hope you liked it

106
00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.720
and I will see you in the next lecture.

